⚠️ Key Constraints & Risks
1. Hugging Face Space Limitations
Cold starts and long-running processes (like browser automation) are not ideal.
No persistent storage between runs — persona JSONs won’t persist unless saved externally or embedded in state.
No direct access to files unless using huggingface_hub or gradio state.
GPU limits — WebVoyager likely uses a browser, which may not run well on T4 GPUs (common in HF Spaces).
2. WebVoyager’s Architecture
WebVoyager is a browser automation agent (likely using Playwright or Selenium).
It probably runs in a separate process or container.
Its system prompt is likely hardcoded or configured at startup — modifying it dynamically requires restarting the agent or injecting config at runtime.
3. TinyTroupe’s Output Format
The /generate_personas endpoint returns a list of TinyPerson instances — likely JSON.
We need to parse this and extract:
Persona name
Background
Goals
Language style (tone, syntax, etc.)
Possibly a system prompt template
4. Styling Integration
You want to style the agent’s thoughts/actions using TinyTroupe’s language model.
This implies post-processing of WebVoyager’s output — or modifying the agent’s LLM prompt to include stylistic instructions.
🧩 Proposed Integration Architecture
We’ll break this into 3 components:

✅ Component 1: Persona Generator (TinyTroupe API)
Use gradio_client to call harvesthealth/tiny_factory with inputs.
Parse returned JSON → extract persona data.
Store persona in memory or state (for current session).
✅ Component 2: System Prompt Injector
Modify WebVoyager’s system prompt to include:
